The recorder part of the voice-messages epic (#120), squashed from the
incremental patch series. Gated behind experimentalAudioPlayerControls
(off = the previous recorder byte-identical: no picker/warning, no
deviceId constraint, no SilenceDetector, the OFF path keeps the aborting
1s NO_INPUT alert). The getUserMedia + ScriptProcessorNode -> real-time
LAME MP3 pipeline is preserved (no MediaRecorder: iOS can't play
Chrome's Opus/WebM).

- Pause/resume: a gate in onaudioprocess stops feeding the encoder, so
  resume continues into the SAME encoder — one contiguous MP3, no
  re-init. The timer banks only active time (pure TimerState, no
  per-tick slop); the live waveform freezes while paused. Mic stream
  stays live while paused (ponytail: OS indicator stays on; upgrade path
  track.enabled).
- Preview before send: stop shows the draft in the shared custom player
  (blob: URL; peaks work via fetch, position persistence is skipped for
  blob: at the store level) with send / re-record / discard. Focus lands
  on Send (the composer input is unmounted while previewing); the
  preview element and the voice singleton pause each other.
- Press-and-hold: hold to record, release to preview; slide left past
  80px to cancel, up past 60px to lock hands-free; a <300ms tap still
  toggles. Pure classifyGesture(); pointer events with capture. The
  context menu is suppressed for the WHOLE gesture via a window-level
  listener (the mic button unmounts ~100ms into a hold, before the OS
  ~500ms long-press would hit its onContextMenu); gesture listeners are
  torn down on unmount too, not only pointerup/cancel.
- Abortable start (KEEP when resolving conflicts): onRecordingStart
  awaits the permission prompt, so a generation counter — bumped on
  cancel/stop and each new start, snapshotted at entry, re-checked after
  every await and before opening the mic — prevents a cancelled gesture
  or double pointerdown from re-opening the mic / leaving two
  MicRecorders; a getUserMedia resolving after being superseded has its
  stream stopped.
- Original-audio toggle (voiceRecordingOriginalAudio, default off):
  requests noiseSuppression/echoCancellation/autoGainControl off —
  advisory, so track.getSettings() is read back and unsupported setups
  say so instead of pretending.
- Mic picker (shown only with >1 audioinput; list refreshed on each menu
  open, ponytail: no devicechange listener): labels fall back to
  "Microphone N", the active device is read from the open track's
  getSettings, and a compact live level renders on the ACTIVE row only,
  driven by the recorder's existing RMS — no extra getUserMedia per
  inactive mic (Jitsi's pattern). Choice persists as
  voiceRecordingDeviceId (string|undefined — the settings persistence
  only round-trips scalars, undefined = system default). A stale
  persisted id ({deviceId:{exact}} throws OverconstrainedError) falls
  back to the system default instead of failing the recording, and the
  UI clears the stored setting; resolveDeviceId() is the pure decision.
- Hot-switch mid-take: MicRecorder.switchDevice() opens the new stream
  FIRST (a failed switch keeps the take on the old device), then swaps
  the source node feeding the same processor/encoder — MP3 stays
  contiguous. A dedicated `swapping` flag gates frames during the swap
  (the user's paused flag stays authoritative — do NOT multiplex it)
  and doubles as a reentrancy guard; the alive-check is on
  context.state, so a stop during the getUserMedia await stops the
  just-opened stream instead of leaking it.
- Inline no-sound hint replaces the aborting alert on the ON path: a
  pure SilenceDetector reports after ~3s of continuous near-silence,
  does NOT abort, clears when signal returns, and sits next to the
  picker (the remedy). Paused frames aren't fed and the detector resets
  on resume, so a pause can't back-date the quiet window. Hint color is
  --colorDanger (AA on all themes).
- Narrow viewports (<=500px): the recording row wraps with Cancel/OK
  grouped right (display:contents wrapper — zero effect on wide
  viewports), preview controls take their own line, the mic-picker menu
  clamps to the viewport above the composer. The <=400px live-waveform
  hide uses a compound selector — a bare class was silently overridden
  by an equal-specificity later rule. Upstream's 85px width clamp is
  scoped to .cancel/.stopRecording so it can't crush the picker labels.

SilenceDetector transitions, gesture thresholds, constraint honesty,
label fallback and stale-deviceId decisions are mocha-tested
(audioRecorder suite). The contiguous-MP3 invariant needs a live
WebAudio pipeline — covered by scripts/shot-voice-player.mjs, not the
unit suite.

web-app: fix recorder gesture precedence, AudioContext leak, phone margins

Three follow-up fixes to the voice recorder, found reviewing the epic
after it landed.

1. Gesture: a diagonal up-AND-left "throw it away" flick locked the
   recording hands-free instead of cancelling it. The live pointermove
   handler locked the instant dy crossed LOCK_DY and set active=false,
   so the pointerup path — which runs classifyGesture, and classifyGesture
   checks cancel *before* lock — never ran. The unit-tested cancel-wins
   precedence was unreachable in the real gesture. Fix: the live handler
   now delegates to classifyGesture and live-locks only when it returns
   'lock', so cancel-over-lock holds on the move path too (covered by the
   existing "cancel wins over lock on a diagonal" case).

2. AudioContext leak: start() opens an AudioContext up front, before
   getUserMedia. On a gUM rejection (permission denied, or the stale-device
   fallback's retry also failing) the caller's error path does not call
   stop(), and stop() wouldn't close the context anyway — its teardown is
   gated on a microphone/processor that never got wired. Repeated failed
   attempts leaked contexts until the browser's concurrent-AudioContext cap
   (~6 in Chrome) started throwing. Fix: start() closes and nulls its own
   context on the reject path.

3. Phone-layout margins: the max-width:500px overrides for .cancel /
   .stopRecording are written outside .audioRecorder, while the base block
   nests them under it. So .cancel/.stopRecording (0,1,0) lose to
   .audioRecorder .cancel (0,2,0) for margin-inline-start, and the doubled
   .stopRecording.stopRecording (0,2,0) still loses to
   .audioRecorder .stopRecording.stopRecording (0,3,0) for margin-inline-end
   — the narrow-viewport margins silently didn't apply either way. Fix:
   prefix all three with .audioRecorder so specificity ties and later
   source order wins.

web-app: move "original audio" out of the recorder into settings

The toggle was a pill inside the recording row, which is the one place
it cannot do anything: the row only exists while a take is running, and
the setting is read in start() when the MicRecorder is built, so it
"applies to the next recording". Using it meant starting a take you
didn't want, toggling, cancelling, and recording again.

Its label also swapped nouns by state — "Voice quality" when off,
"Original audio" when on — so it read as two different controls rather
than one control with two states, and "quality" suggested bitrate, the
one thing it does not touch. It flips three advisory getUserMedia
constraints (noiseSuppression, echoCancellation, autoGainControl); the
encoder config is identical either way.

It is a persisted preference, so it belongs with the other persisted
preferences: Settings -> Experimental -> Composer, as a plain switch
with a stable label that says what you would hear rather than naming
the three DSP terms.

Drops the pill, the now-dead honored-readback state in the recorder (no
surface left to show it — a following patch gives it one in settings),
and the four locale strings that went with it.

